      title: "The Colonies in 1750",
      body: `## A society growing faster than any in the world

In 1700 about 250,000 people of European and African descent lived in the English colonies of mainland North America. By 1750 there were about 1.2 million; by 1775, about 2.5 million. No other society in the Western world grew so fast. Benjamin Franklin, who studied the matter with characteristic curiosity, calculated in 1751 that the colonial population was doubling every twenty to twenty-five years, and predicted that within a century more Englishmen would live in America than in England.

The reasons were land and youth. Land was abundant and cheap compared with Europe, so young people could marry early and support large families. The typical colonial woman married in her early twenties and bore seven or eight children, most of whom survived. Immigration added hundreds of thousands more: Scots-Irish Presbyterians from Ulster, Germans from the Rhineland and Switzerland, English and Scottish servants and convicts, and—the largest stream of all—Africans carried across the Atlantic against their will.

The result was a population unlike that of Britain in two respects. It was very young: about half the colonists were under sixteen. And it was diverse. By 1775 perhaps half of the colonists south of New England were not of English descent. Pennsylvania was perhaps one-third German. Scots-Irish settlers filled the backcountry from Pennsylvania down the Great Wagon Road into the Carolina hills. New York kept the Dutch character of its founding, with Dutch churches and Dutch-speaking farmers in the Hudson Valley. And about one person in five in the colonies as a whole was of African descent, almost all of them enslaved.

## Four regions

Historians often divide the colonies into regions, each with its own economy and society.

**New England**—Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island, and New Hampshire—was the most English and most homogeneous region. Its people lived mostly in compact towns of family farms, organized around Congregational churches and town meetings. Its soil was poor, so many New Englanders turned to the sea: fishing, shipbuilding, and trade with the West Indies, where they sold fish and lumber to feed and house the enslaved people on sugar plantations. Boston, with about 16,000 people, was the region's capital.

The **Middle Colonies**—New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and Delaware—were the most diverse. Their rich farmland made them the breadbasket of the empire, exporting wheat and flour. Philadelphia, with perhaps 30,000 people by 1775, was the largest city in British America, and New York was close behind. Pennsylvania, founded by the Quaker William Penn, practiced a religious toleration that drew settlers of every denomination.

The **Chesapeake**—Virginia and Maryland—was the land of tobacco. Great planters such as the Byrds, Carters, and Lees ruled from brick mansions over plantations worked by enslaved Africans, while smaller farmers grew tobacco with their own hands and perhaps a few enslaved workers. Virginia was the most populous colony.

The **Lower South**—the Carolinas and Georgia—was the most like the Caribbean. In the Lowcountry around Charleston, rice and indigo plantations produced great wealth for a small planter elite and were worked by an enslaved majority: in South Carolina, Africans and their descendants outnumbered Europeans by nearly two to one.

## An empire of goods

What bound these different regions together was not a common government—there was none—but a common connection to Britain. Each colony traded far more with Britain than with its neighbors. Ships carried tobacco, rice, wheat, timber, and furs east across the Atlantic, and brought back manufactured goods: cloth, ceramics, tea, glass, paper, paint, books, and a thousand other things.

In the middle decades of the eighteenth century this trade grew explosively. Colonial imports from Britain roughly doubled between 1740 and 1770. The historian T. H. Breen has called this the "consumer revolution": ordinary colonists, not only the rich, began to drink tea from china cups, sleep on manufactured sheets, and read newspapers printed on imported paper. The same goods appeared in shops from Boston to Savannah. Colonists who had never met could recognize one another as fellow consumers of the same British things.

This mattered, Breen argues, because it gave colonists a shared experience, and later a shared weapon. When the imperial crisis came, the colonists' most powerful form of resistance would be the refusal to buy British goods—a weapon that only worked because so many of them had come to depend on those goods.

## Becoming more British

It is tempting to look back from 1776 and see the colonists drifting steadily away from Britain. In fact, in the middle of the eighteenth century, they were becoming more British, not less. Historians call this process **anglicization**. Colonial elites built houses in the Georgian style, read the London magazines, imitated English manners, sent their sons to be educated in England or Scotland, and modeled their courts and assemblies on English institutions. Colonial lawyers studied English law; colonial clergymen read English sermons.

Print accelerated the process. In 1704 there was one newspaper in the colonies, the *Boston News-Letter*; by 1765 there were about twenty-five, most of them reprinting news, essays, and debates from London. Colonial readers followed the doings of Parliament and the court almost as closely as Londoners did, if a few weeks later. The same newspapers would soon carry the arguments of resistance from one colony to another.

Above all, colonists took pride in being British subjects. They celebrated the king's birthday with bonfires and toasts. They believed that the British constitution was the best in the world, and that its genius was liberty. To understand why they rebelled, we must first understand why, in 1760, they would have found the idea of independence absurd.

> **Pause and consider.** Colonists grew more British in the very decades before they declared independence. How might growing closeness to Britain—economic, cultural, political—have made the breach more likely rather than less?`,
