      title: "Slavery and Freedom in British America",
      body: `## Slavery in every colony

In 1750 slavery was legal in every one of the thirteen colonies. It was not a Southern peculiarity but an American institution. Enslaved people worked on wharves in Boston, in households and workshops in New York and Philadelphia, on farms in New Jersey and on Long Island. New York City had one of the largest urban enslaved populations in the colonies. Rhode Island merchants were among the leading slave traders in North America. By 1770 there were roughly 460,000 enslaved people in the thirteen colonies—about one in five of the population.

But slavery's weight was very unequal. In New England, enslaved people were perhaps 3 percent of the population. In the Chesapeake they were about 40 percent; in South Carolina, about 60 percent. In the plantation colonies, slavery was not one institution among many but the foundation of the economy and the social order.

## The Chesapeake and the Lowcountry

Slavery had grown in the Chesapeake in the late seventeenth century, as planters replaced English indentured servants with enslaved Africans (a story told in AMH 110). By the mid-eighteenth century, the enslaved population of Virginia and Maryland was growing by natural increase, as families formed and children were born. Many enslaved people in the Chesapeake were American-born, spoke English, and worked in small groups on tobacco farms alongside—or under the close supervision of—their owners.

The Lowcountry of South Carolina and Georgia was different. Rice cultivation, a brutal labor in flooded fields, demanded large gangs of workers, and planters imported Africans in great numbers. Many came from rice-growing regions of West Africa, and their knowledge may have contributed to the techniques of rice planting. Because the climate drove planters away from their plantations for much of the year, enslaved people often worked under the **task system**: once a day's assigned task was complete, the rest of the day was theirs to tend their own gardens and livestock. Lowcountry slaves lived in large communities, often with few white people nearby, and they preserved more of their African languages and cultures; the Gullah language of the Sea Islands is one result.

## Resistance

Enslaved people resisted their bondage in every way they could: by working slowly, breaking tools, feigning illness, running away, and, occasionally, by rebellion.

The largest rebellion in the mainland colonies before the Revolution broke out near Charleston in September 1739. A group of enslaved men, many probably from the Kingdom of Kongo and some perhaps Catholic and experienced with weapons, seized guns from a store near the Stono River, killed the storekeepers, and marched south toward Spanish Florida, where the Spanish governor had promised freedom to runaway slaves from the British colonies. Their numbers grew as they marched. The colonial militia caught them the same day; dozens of rebels and some two dozen white colonists were killed. The **Stono Rebellion** terrified South Carolina's planters, who passed a harsh new slave code in 1740 and imposed a duty to slow the importation of Africans for a time.

Two years later, in New York City, a series of fires led to accusations of a slave conspiracy to burn the city. In a trial that recalls the Salem witch trials, more than thirty people—most of them enslaved Black men—were executed, some burned at the stake. Historians still debate whether there was any conspiracy at all.

## Free people of color

Not every person of African descent in the colonies was enslaved. Small communities of free Black people lived in the northern cities and in the Chesapeake, some descended from Africans who had gained their freedom in the seventeenth century before the laws of slavery hardened, others freed by their owners or by purchasing their own freedom. Their position was precarious. Colonial laws restricted their right to vote, to bear arms, to testify against whites, and to move freely, and they lived with the constant danger of being kidnapped and sold into slavery. Yet these communities would produce some of the earliest Black voices in American letters and politics, such as the poet Phillis Wheatley, freed in Boston in the 1770s, whom you may meet in POE 310.

## The paradox

The colonists who were about to proclaim that all men are created equal held hundreds of thousands of people in bondage. This is not merely a moral irony for later generations to notice; it is central to the history.

The historian Edmund S. Morgan, in *American Slavery, American Freedom* (1975), argued that slavery and freedom in Virginia grew up together. By replacing a turbulent class of poor white servants with enslaved Africans, Virginia's planters reduced the danger of rebellion among poor whites, who could now share with the rich the status of free men and a common contempt for Black slaves. The equality of white men, Morgan argued, rested on the inequality of Black people. Virginians could celebrate liberty in part because they had made slavery the lot of others.

Other historians have emphasized how the colonists' language of liberty was saturated with images of slavery. When patriots spoke of Britain's attempts to "enslave" them, they knew exactly what slavery meant: they saw it every day. And as we will see, the Revolution's language of natural rights would be taken up by enslaved people themselves, who petitioned colonial legislatures for their freedom in the very words of the patriots.

## Slavery and the empire

Slavery also tied the colonies to the wider British Empire. The most valuable British colonies were not on the mainland but in the Caribbean—Jamaica, Barbados, the Leeward Islands—where enslaved Africans produced sugar, the most lucrative commodity in the Atlantic economy. The mainland colonies fed those islands. New England's fish, the Middle Colonies' flour, and the Chesapeake's timber went south; sugar, molasses, and rum came north. Rum distilled from West Indian molasses was carried to West Africa to purchase captives. The prosperity of colonial merchants in Newport and Boston, as well as planters in Virginia, rested on the labor of the enslaved.

> **Pause and consider.** Why might colonists who held slaves have been especially sensitive to any threat of "enslavement" by Britain? What did they understand by the word?`,
