      title: "Native Nations and Imperial Rivals",
      body: `## A continent of many powers

A map of North America in 1750 drawn by a European would show three great empires: British along the Atlantic coast, French along the St. Lawrence and Mississippi rivers, and Spanish in Florida, Texas, and New Mexico. But this map would mislead. Most of the continent was controlled by none of them. It belonged to dozens of Native nations, who were not passive victims of European rivalry but powers in their own right, pursuing their own interests through trade, diplomacy, and war.

The British colonies, though populous, hugged the coast. West of the Appalachian Mountains lay the **Ohio Valley** and the Great Lakes, home to the Shawnee, Delaware (Lenape), Miami, Wyandot, Ottawa, and other peoples, many of them refugees from earlier wars and displacements. To the north lay the most formidable Native power in the East, the **Haudenosaunee**, or Iroquois Confederacy.

## The Iroquois Confederacy

The Haudenosaunee—the "People of the Longhouse"—were a league of five nations in what is now upstate New York: the Mohawk, Oneida, Onondaga, Cayuga, and Seneca. In the early eighteenth century the Tuscarora, driven north from the Carolinas by war with colonists, joined them as a sixth nation. The League had been founded, according to Iroquois tradition, by the Peacemaker and Hiawatha, who ended the wars among the five nations and established a Great Law of Peace, with a council of chiefs meeting at Onondaga.

The Iroquois were skilled diplomats. Through a series of treaties known as the **Covenant Chain**, they allied themselves with the English colonies, especially New York, while maintaining trade and diplomacy with the French. By playing the two empires against each other, they preserved their independence and influence. In the 1701 "Grand Settlement," they made peace with the French while maintaining their English alliance, adopting a policy of neutrality that served them well for half a century.

## New France

The French empire in North America was vast in extent but thin in population. **New France** stretched from the mouth of the St. Lawrence through the Great Lakes and down the Mississippi to New Orleans. But it had fewer than 80,000 settlers in 1750, compared with more than a million in the British colonies. Most lived in the St. Lawrence Valley around Quebec and Montreal.

The French compensated for their small numbers through alliances with Native peoples. French fur traders lived among Native communities, learned their languages, and married Native women. French officials gave gifts, mediated disputes, and participated in Native ceremonies. The historian Richard White, in *The Middle Ground* (1991), described the Great Lakes region as a place where French and Algonquian peoples, neither able to dominate the other, created a shared world of accommodation, ritual, and mutual misunderstanding. The British, whose settlers wanted land rather than furs, found it far harder to make such alliances. To Native peoples, the French were traders and allies; the British were farmers who would take their land.

## The Ohio Valley

By the 1740s, the empires were colliding in the Ohio Valley. Pennsylvania fur traders had pushed west across the mountains, trading with the Native peoples of the region. Virginia land speculators—among them the Ohio Company, whose investors included members of the Washington family—claimed the same lands for settlement. The French, alarmed at the threat to the link between their Canadian and Louisiana colonies, began building a chain of forts from Lake Erie toward the forks of the Ohio, where the Allegheny and Monongahela rivers meet (the site of modern Pittsburgh).

In 1753 the governor of Virginia, Robert Dinwiddie, sent a twenty-one-year-old militia officer named **George Washington** to warn the French to leave. They refused. The next year, Washington returned with a small force. The war that followed would decide the fate of the continent.

## The Southeast

In the South, the great Native powers were the **Cherokee**, in the mountains of the Carolinas and Georgia, and the **Creek** (Muscogee) confederacy in the interior of Georgia and Alabama, along with the Choctaw and Chickasaw farther west. They, too, traded deerskins with Charleston merchants and balanced the British against the French in Louisiana and the Spanish in Florida.

## Native strategies

It is important to see this conflict from the perspective of the Native nations of the Ohio Valley. They did not want either empire to dominate them. They traded with both, accepted gifts from both, and sought to keep both at a distance. Their great fear was that a victory by either side would leave them at the mercy of the winner. As events would show, that fear was justified. When the French were driven from North America, the Native nations of the interior lost the ability to play one empire against another, and faced a British empire—and its land-hungry colonists—alone.

> **Pause and consider.** Why would the defeat of France be a disaster for Native peoples who had often fought against the French?`,
